<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adhemar of Le Puy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1098">1098</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Adhemar of Monteil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adhemar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy</a>, also called Adhemar of Monteil, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Le Puy and the papal legate appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. He travelled with the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond of Saint-Gilles</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, and acted as spiritual leader and mediator among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> princes. He died of disease at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in August 1098, before the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adhemar-of-le-puy.jpg" alt="Adhemar of Le Puy" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adh%C3%A9mar_de_Monteil_%C3%A0_Antioche.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adhemar, also known as Adémar, Aimar or Aelarz, was born around 1045 into the family of the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valentinois</a> and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Puy-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velay">Velay</a> before 1087. He was an advocate of the Gregorian Reform, and his supporters included the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iv-of-toulouse">Raymond of Saint-Gilles</a>, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. He is said to have gone on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 1080s. His brother William Hugh of Monteil also took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>. Adhemar most likely met Urban II when the pope visited Puy in August 1095.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a> in 1095 Adhemar showed great zeal for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and there is evidence that Urban had conferred with him before the council. On 27 November 1095 Urban named him apostolic legate and appointed him to lead the expedition. He spent the next year raising money and recruiting men, and on 15 August 1096 he left with Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>'s army. Raymond and the other leaders often quarrelled over leadership, but Adhemar was always recognized as the spiritual leader. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a>, Pecheneg mercenaries beat and robbed him until crusaders noticed the disturbance and saved him. He then fell ill at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and rejoined the army later.</p><p>He negotiated with Alexius I Comnenus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, restored some discipline at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> and played a crucial part at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a>. At the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> he sustained morale with fasts and observances, among them a three-day fast after an earthquake, and ordered the crusaders to shave and wear a cross so that they would not attack one another by mistake. During the siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha</a> he had the gates locked so that panicking crusaders could not leave. He was sceptical of Peter Bartholomew's discovery of the Holy Lance, since such a relic was already held at Constantinople, but allowed the army to believe in it if it raised morale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Adhemar fell sick in June 1098 and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha</a>'s defeat, organized a council to settle the leadership disputes. He died on 1 August 1098, probably of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, and was buried in the Basilica of St Peter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. The disputes among the nobles went unresolved and the march to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> was delayed for months, though the ordinary soldiers continued to regard him as a leader. Several crusaders reported visions of him. Peter Bartholomew claimed he had spent days in hell for his scepticism, and Peter Desiderius said that at Jerusalem Adhemar told him to have the crusaders fast and process around the walls. Jerusalem was taken in 1099.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adhemar of Le Puy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Raymond IV, also known as Raymond of Saint-Gilles, took the cross in 1095 and led probably the largest contingent of the First Crusade, accompanied by the papal legate <b>Adhemar of Le Puy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition has ascribed it to the eleventh-century German monk Hermann of Reichenau, and also to Bernard of Clairvaux, Peter of Compostela and <b>Adhemar of Le Puy</b>, among others, but most musicologists regard it as anonymous.</blockquote><a href="/songs/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Valentinois, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.79,5.02&amp;year=1088" aria-label="Valentinois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4670 581 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adhemar of Le Puy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5120 851h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5120 851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.79,5.02&amp;year=1088">What was here in 1088? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
